# Architecture

## Stack

- Language: TypeScript (both `client` and `server`)
- Client: React 19 + Vite 8, scaffolded with `npm create vite@latest -- --template react-ts`
- Server: Node.js + Express 5
- Build tool: Vite (client), `tsc` (server)
- Package manager: npm, using npm workspaces for the monorepo
- Runtime: Node.js >= 22 (LTS)
- Module system: ESM (`"type": "module"` on every `package.json`)
- Serving model: **one process, one port in production**. The Express server is the only process
  that runs: it exposes the API and, from the same origin and the same port, serves the client's
  Vite production build (`client/dist`) plus the SPA history fallback. Vite is a build tool at that
  point, not a runtime. The two-process flow (Vite dev server + Express) survives **for local
  development only**, for HMR.

## Structure

```
DockerClient/
├── package.json          # workspace root: npm workspaces ["client", "server"]
├── package-lock.json
├── .nvmrc                 # pins Node 22
├── .gitignore              # root-level ignores (node_modules, dist, .env, ...)
├── client/                 # generated by `npm create vite@latest client -- --template react-ts`
│   ├── package.json         # name: "client"
│   ├── index.html
│   ├── vite.config.ts
│   ├── tsconfig.json         # references tsconfig.app.json / tsconfig.node.json
│   ├── tsconfig.app.json
│   ├── tsconfig.node.json
│   ├── .oxlintrc.json         # lint config (oxlint, bundled by the Vite template)
│   ├── .gitignore              # Vite-specific ignores
│   ├── public/                  # static assets (favicon.svg, icons.svg)
│   └── src/
│       ├── main.tsx               # React entrypoint, mounts <App />
│       ├── App.tsx                 # default component from the Vite template
│       ├── App.css
│       ├── index.css
│       └── assets/
└── server/                  # hand-created, no idiomatic non-interactive TS+Express generator exists
    ├── package.json          # name: "server"
    ├── tsconfig.json           # NodeNext module/resolution, strict, outDir dist
    └── src/
        └── index.ts              # Express app bootstrap; GET /health -> { status: "ok" }
```

The tree above is the **init-time snapshot**. Since then the internal structure of each package has
grown as development required it (feature areas under `server/src/*`, the UI library under
`client/src/ui/`, the test trees under `server/test/` and `client/e2e/`); it is not re-listed here,
per the "no invented structure" principle — this file records the architecture, not an inventory.

### Serving topology

```
production (one process, one port — default 3000)
  browser ──► Express (server/dist/index.js)
                ├── GET /health                     → liveness
                ├── /api/*                          → API routers (unchanged)
                ├── GET /api/events                 → SSE stream (unchanged)
                ├── HTTP upgrade on /api/…          → WebSocket sessions (unchanged)
                ├── static assets                   → client/dist (hashed files under /assets)
                └── SPA history fallback            → client/dist/index.html

development (two processes, two ports)
  browser ──► Vite dev server :5173 ── HMR, index.html, sources
                └── proxy /api (ws: true) ──► Express :3000
```

- `client/dist` is a **build artifact** consumed by the server at runtime; it is git-ignored and is
  produced by `npm run build -w client`. Nothing is copied into the server workspace: the server
  resolves the directory from its own module URL (`server/dist/index.js` → `../../client/dist`), so
  the path holds whatever the working directory is, and an override env var
  (`VEXEL_CLIENT_DIST`) lets a packaged deployment point elsewhere.
- Serving the client is mounted **last**, after `/health` and every `/api/*` router, and the history
  fallback answers only `GET`/`HEAD` requests that are not under `/api`. An unknown API route
  therefore still 404s as JSON instead of being answered with `index.html`.
- The WebSocket upgrade path is untouched: it is handled on the `server.on("upgrade")` hook of the
  `http.Server`, which no Express middleware — static or fallback — can intercept.
- When `client/dist` is absent (a checkout that has never been built, the dev flow where Vite owns
  the client), the server logs it once and runs API-only. A missing build is not a fatal error.

## Dependencies

### client (`client/package.json`)
- `react`, `react-dom` — UI framework, requested by the human.
- `vite`, `@vitejs/plugin-react` — build tool / dev server, requested by the human ("React + Vite").
- `typescript` — requested by the human.
- `oxlint` — linter bundled by the current `create-vite` `react-ts` template (replaces ESLint in
  this template generation); kept as-is since it is part of the idiomatic scaffold output.
- `@types/react`, `@types/react-dom`, `@types/node` — type definitions for the above.

### server (`server/package.json`)
- `express` — server framework (assumption, see below). Also the **static file server and SPA host**
  in production: `express.static` and `res.sendFile` ship with Express 5, so serving the client adds
  **no new dependency** (no `serve-static`, no `sirv`, no reverse proxy in front).
- `ws` — WebSocket sessions for container exec/attach, handled on the HTTP `upgrade` hook.
- `typescript` — requested by the human.
- `tsx` — TypeScript runner used for the `dev` script (`tsx watch src/index.ts`); avoids a manual
  build step during local development.
- `@types/express`, `@types/node` — type definitions for the above.

## Conventions

- Package/folder naming: kebab-case, role-based (`client`, `server`) inside the npm workspace; root
  package name `docker-client`, derived in kebab-case from the working directory name.
- Source code, identifiers, comments: English only (see `code-language` convention).
- Each workspace package owns its `tsconfig.json`; no shared base config was introduced (not part
  of what the idiomatic scaffolds produced, and only two packages exist so far — avoids inventing
  structure not yet needed).
- Server entrypoint (`server/src/index.ts`) exposes `GET /health` returning `{ "status": "ok" }`,
  mounts every `/api/*` router, and — last of all — the client's static build and the SPA fallback.
  **Mount order is load-bearing**: anything registered after the history fallback would be
  unreachable, and anything registered before `/api` could shadow the API.
- The client is served from the **same origin and the same port** as the API. The client therefore
  keeps calling `/api/...` with relative URLs in both flows: through the Vite proxy in development,
  directly in production. No base URL, no CORS configuration, no origin in the client code.
- Client entrypoint/component (`src/main.tsx`, `src/App.tsx`) is exactly what `create-vite`
  generates for the `react-ts` template (demo counter component); no domain logic added.

## Commands

Run from the repository root (npm workspaces):

- Install all dependencies: `npm install`
- **Run the whole application (one process, one port): `npm start`** — chains
  `npm run build && npm run serve`, i.e. builds the client, builds the server, then runs the single
  Express process which serves API + client on `http://localhost:3000` (override with `PORT`).
  This is the command the human asked for: one command, one process, the complete app.
- Serve an existing build without rebuilding: `npm run serve` (equivalent to `npm start -w server`,
  i.e. `node dist/index.js`). Use it when only the server has to be restarted; it fails soft and
  runs API-only if `client/dist` has never been built.
- Local development (two processes, HMR — **development only**):
  - `npm run dev:server` (equivalent to `npm run dev -w server`) — Express on port 3000, watch mode.
  - `npm run dev:client` (equivalent to `npm run dev -w client`) — Vite on port 5173, proxying
    `/api` (with `ws: true`) to port 3000, so origin-relative calls, the SSE stream and the exec
    WebSocket behave exactly as they do in the single-process flow.
  - Both are needed together; `npm start` needs neither.
- Build both: `npm run build` (`tsc -b && vite build` for client, `tsc -p tsconfig.json` for server).
  **The client builds first, on purpose**: the server process serves the output of that build.
- Low-verbosity variants (what the agents use): prefix with `-s` to silence npm's own script echo —
  `npm run build -s`, `npm start -s`, `npm run test -s`. `tsc` and `vite build` are already quiet on
  success; the test scripts already pin dot reporters.
- Lint client: `npm run lint` (`oxlint`, low-verbosity by default)
- Server tests: `npm run test -w server` — chains the four passes below, in this order:
  - `npm run test:typecheck -w server` — typechecks the test tree (`tsc -p tsconfig.test.json`,
    `noEmit`, same strictness as the build). `tsconfig.json` includes `src` alone so the production
    build stays free of test files, and `tsx` strips types without checking them: without this pass
    a test file could drift from the modules it imports — a wrong import path, a renamed export, a
    stale response shape — and nothing would say so, since the tests still run. Runs first, before
    any daemon work, so that kind of drift fails in seconds.
  - `npm run test:unit -w server` — pure unit tests, no daemon involved
  - `npm run test:api -w server` — REST-level tests against the real daemon, files in parallel;
    every fixture it creates carries the `vexel.test.run` ownership label
  - `npm run test:destructive` (repository root) — only the tests that act on the whole host, named
    one by one in `scripts/destructive-tests.mjs`. They are not a pass of their own: they run inside
    `test:api` and the e2e suite like every other file, and this command exists so they can be run
    alone, and so the suite can be run without them touching the operator's machine
  - `npm run test:sweep -w server` — removes the containers, volumes, networks and images left
    behind by a killed run; scoped to the ownership label, so it never touches an object the
    operator created. Runs automatically before the two daemon-backed passes.
  - `npm run test:images -w server` — the first of the two preliminary steps of a daemon-backed
    pass: puts on the daemon what has to be on the daemon. The only step of a run allowed to reach
    Docker Hub, and only for what is genuinely not there yet. Runs automatically before the two
    daemon-backed passes.
  - `npm run test:registry -w server` — the second, and the reason no test ever reaches Docker Hub:
    it brings up the run's own throwaway `registry:2` and seeds it with every image the tests pull.
    A registry exposed on the internet occasionally does not answer, and this suite has lost whole
    specs to exactly that; a refusal at a preliminary step stops a run before it starts, which says
    what it is, while the same refusal inside a test says nothing about the product. `alpine:3.20`
    and `moby/buildkit:buildx-stable-1` are seeded from the daemon's own copy (a tag and a push, no
    network at all) and restored from there whenever a prune removes them; `vexel-test-tiny:1`, the
    single-layer image, is built `FROM scratch` and fetched from nowhere, and a copy of it is
    published for the tests that contract "a reference missing locally is fetched first";
    `registry:2` is the one irreducible exception, since it is what the registry itself is run from,
    so it comes from Hub if the daemon does not hold it. The container is named once per machine and
    carries the ownership label, so `test:sweep` removes it — and with it anything the daemon pulled
    out of it — after a killed run. Every step is idempotent: an already-prepared registry is the
    normal case, so a single test file run directly gets the same arrangement rather than a second
    one. The two pulled images are shared infrastructure — no ownership label, and the sweep leaves
    them alone. One definition, `server/test/support/base-images.ts`, and the e2e suite runs these
    two commands themselves from its global setup rather than reimplementing them.
  - Both daemon-backed passes run against their own `VEXEL_DATA_DIR` under the system temp
    directory, so the operator's `~/.vexel` preferences and analysis cache are never written to.
    That directory is **emptied before every single test** — all of it, not one namespace: the
    preferences, the console history, the analysis cache and its index. A `beforeEach` the passes
    preload with `--import ./test/support/fresh-data-dir.ts`, so no test file can forget it. Not
    tidiness: `persistence-routes.test.ts` asked for the default preferences and was handed a
    `selectedContext` another test had set, and the analysis cache — keyed by content digest, never
    evicting (REQ-113) — hands the second test to analyse the same content the first one's result
    and skips the extraction it exists to drive, a test that has stopped testing anything and passes
    while doing so. The cache goes through the store's own `clear()`; the other namespaces have no
    such API and their files are removed, which `local-store.ts` reads correctly by design (per-call
    read, fallback to defaults). The directory itself is never removed — the store resolves it once,
    at import. `test:reset-data-dir` remains the way to empty it by hand.

    This **reverses** an earlier decision, recorded here so it is not reinstated from the old
    argument. The directory used to be kept warm between runs, said to take the suite "from minutes
    to well over ten" if lost. Measured: `npm run test` 7m23s before against 7m26s after, full e2e
    13m21s–14m12s before against 13m30s and 14m07s after — inside the run-to-run spread, not
    minutes. Meanwhile it had reached 3.2 GB across 1936 artefacts, every run building fixture
    images with content of their own and nothing evicting. It now never holds more than the running
    test put there.
- Client tests: `npm run test -w client` (test-tree typecheck + UI-boundary check + `vitest run`) and
  `npm run test:e2e -w client` (Playwright).
  - `npm run test:typecheck -w client` — the counterpart of the server's pass, and for the same
    reason: `tsconfig.app.json` includes `src` alone, while vitest and Playwright both strip types
    without checking them, so `test/` and `e2e/` were checked by nobody. `tsc -p tsconfig.test.json`,
    `noEmit`, same strictness as the app build, covering `src`, `test/`, `e2e/` and the two test
    configs. Runs first, before the UI-boundary check and any test, so drift between a spec and the
    modules it imports fails in seconds. It is the only thing that checks the e2e tree at all: those
    specs are never run by `npm run test`.
  Use the workspace-scoped scripts, not `vitest`/
  `playwright` invoked by hand with a `--root`: `ui-conformance-check.test.ts` resolves the
  conformance script from `process.cwd()`, so running the binaries from the repository root fails 8
  tests that pass under the npm scripts.
- Whole suite: `npm run test` from the repository root chains server then client. The e2e suite runs with a **single worker on purpose** — every spec drives the
  same daemon, so concurrency turns contention into spurious timeouts at no wall-clock gain; see the
  rationale in `client/playwright.config.ts`. Destructive specs run in that same single project,
  beside every other file: each spec re-establishes the base images it needs at the point of use, so
  a prune landing mid-run costs a local restore and nothing else.
- The e2e run isolates itself from the machine it runs on, in two ways set up by
  `client/e2e/support/global-setup.ts` and `playwright.config.ts`: the server under test is pointed
  at a throwaway `VEXEL_DATA_DIR` (emptied and recreated before each run, so the operator's own
  `~/.vexel` preferences and analysis cache are neither read nor written; and emptied again before
  **every single test**, by the automatic fixture in `e2e/support/test.ts` — which is why specs
  import `test` from there rather than from `@playwright/test`), and the run's own
  registry is brought up by running `npm run test:images -w server` and `npm run test:registry -w
  server` — the server passes' own two commands, not a second implementation of them — so no spec
  spends its own time budget reaching Docker Hub.
  `client/e2e/support/global-teardown.ts` stops that registry when the run ends. **No running process is ever
  reused** (`reuseExistingServer: false`): a reused one would serve whatever build it happens to
  hold, under whatever data directory it was started with, and both failures are silent.
- The rules every test follows — clean up what you create, assume nothing about the daemon's
  contents, inherit nothing from another test — are stated in `CLAUDE.md` ("Tests — non-negotiable
  rule"), alongside the reasons each of them exists.
- In a daemon-backed test, everything that can throw belongs **inside the `try` whose `finally`
  tears down** the HTTP server and the fixtures. Setup left between `await startApp(...)` and the
  `try` is the trap: when it fails, the `finally` never runs, the Express server keeps listening and
  the file never exits — a missing image surfaces as a ten-minute hang instead of a failed
  assertion. Only provably non-throwing statements (a name built from a template literal, a `let`
  declaration, `new AbortController()`) may sit there.
- The e2e suite drives the **delivered single-process form**, and only that one: its single
  `webServer` entry runs the root `npm start` (build the client, build the server, serve both), and
  `baseURL` points at that one origin. No Vite dev server is started and nothing listens on 5173
  during a run. The port is **3100**, set through `PORT`, so a developer's `npm run dev:server` on
  3000 and a run stay out of each other's way; the readiness probe is the server's own `/health`,
  with a timeout sized for a full cold build rather than for a dev server's start-up. The cost is
  accepted and deliberate: every e2e run pays a client build, in the suite's start-up rather than in
  the developer's habits. The development flow is consequently **not covered by any automated
  check** — it survives for manual work with hot reload, and its acceptance is a human one.
- Server production start (after build): `npm start -w server` (`node dist/index.js`) — the single
  process, serving API **and** client. `npm run serve` from the root is the same thing.

## Assumptions

The human decided: client = React + Vite (browser frontend), language = TypeScript on both sides.
Everything below was not specified and was assumed, with rationale:

- **npm as package manager** — ships with Node, zero extra tooling to install; no signal in the
  prompt favoring yarn/pnpm.
- **npm workspaces monorepo with `client/` and `server/` packages** — the prompt explicitly says
  "client server ... app": two cooperating packages is the direct, idiomatic reading, and npm
  workspaces is the built-in way to manage that without extra tooling.
- **Express as the server framework** — the de-facto standard minimal HTTP framework for Node,
  requires no extra decisions (routing style, plugin system) to produce a runnable skeleton.
- **Node.js current LTS (>= 22)** — matches the Node version available in the target environment
  (`v22.13.1`) and is within Node's LTS support window as of the current date (2026-08-05); pinned
  via `.nvmrc` and `engines` in the root `package.json`.
- **ESM (`"type": "module"`)** — the modern default for new Node/TypeScript projects; avoids mixing
  CommonJS and ESM across the two packages.
- **Root package name `docker-client`** — kebab-case derived from the working directory name
  `DockerClient`, per npm's own naming convention; used only as the private workspace root name, not
  as a signal about the application's domain (no domain logic exists yet).

### Single-process serving (2026-08-10)

The human decided: one npm process serves the whole application; Express serves `client/dist` plus
the SPA history fallback; one command at the root builds and serves it on one port; the two-process
Vite flow stays for local development; API surface, WebSocket upgrade and SSE stream unchanged.
Everything below was not specified and was assumed, with rationale:

- **Same port and same `PORT` variable as today (default 3000)** — "one port" means the API's, since
  the API is what already has clients (the e2e suite, `GET /health` probes). Introducing a new port
  or a new variable would break them for no gain.
- **Static assets served straight out of `client/dist`, nothing copied into the server workspace** —
  the human named `client/dist` as the source. A copy step would add a build stage, a second copy of
  every asset and a way for the two to disagree. If a self-contained artifact is ever needed
  (a container image, a published server package), the `VEXEL_CLIENT_DIST` override is the seam that
  makes it a packaging concern rather than an architectural one.
- **The directory is resolved from the server's module URL, not from `process.cwd()`** — the server
  is launched from the repository root (`npm run serve`) and from its own workspace
  (`npm start -w server`); only a module-relative path is the same in both.
- **A missing `client/dist` is not fatal: the server logs it once and runs API-only** — that is
  exactly the state of the development flow, where Vite owns the client, and of a fresh checkout.
  Crashing there would make `npm run dev:server` depend on a client build it does not use.
- **Root `start` = `build` + `serve`, with `serve` kept separate** — the human asked for a single
  command that builds *and* serves, which `npm start` now is. A build-free `npm run serve` remains
  because restarting the process is the frequent operation and rebuilding the client each time would
  cost seconds for nothing.
- **No new dependency, no reverse proxy** — `express.static` and `res.sendFile` are part of
  Express 5. Adding nginx or `serve-static` in front would reintroduce the second process the human
  is removing.
- **The history fallback is restricted to `GET`/`HEAD` outside `/api`** — an unknown API route must
  keep failing as a JSON 404; answering it with `index.html` turns a client bug into a silent
  "renders the app" that no test can catch.
- **The e2e suite targets the delivered single-process form, not the dev flow** — decided by the
  human: removing one of the two servers means everything runs on the one that remains, the tests
  included. One delivered form, verified once, and it is the one that ships. The suite was moved
  rather than duplicated (no second project running the same specs twice, no suite left behind on
  5173), on its own port **3100** with no reuse of a running process, so a developer's server on
  3000 is neither disturbed nor consulted. Accepted consequence: the dev flow is the arrangement now
  left to manual verification.
